(* Beginning of Notebook Content *)
Notebook[{
Cell["FindArrayEquations", "ObjectName",ExpressionUUID->"ba28efe8-de6e-5b4b-9c1b-89ec44ca8e71"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"FindArrayEquations", "[", 
    RowBox[{"ops", ",", " ", "args"}], "]"}], "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"93c91726-ae6d-354c-9321-7a6563aae738"],
 " ",
 "searches for equational identities among all the ways the operations ops \
can be nested over the arguments args, and returns them as a list of \
equations."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"3255955d-b533-1841-9810-f75fee5929fb"],

Cell["Details and Options", "NotesSection",ExpressionUUID->"e0e8c8b6-d43a-9f4d-9ec4-6346aef836c9"],

Cell["\<\
Each operation is given as op -> arity, or as op alone when its arity can be \
read off the first of its definitions. Operations of different arities may be \
mixed.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"0e8b20b2-d7ef-de48-bf62-73bd3553c00e"],

Cell["\<\
The arguments are symbols standing for generic arrays, and may be repeated. \
Only those nestings that use exactly the given arguments are considered, so \
the number of arguments must fit the arities; with a single ternary \
operation, for instance, it must be odd.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"753f6f5f-24f0-9643-bdea-733c43b11879"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "With ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"\"\<Permute\>\"", " ", "->", " ", "True"}], "InlineCode"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"049d798b-c36a-1240-af52-224ddc4b46d9"],
 ", the default, the right-hand side of a candidate equation ranges over \
every permutation of the arguments as well as every nesting. With ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"\"\<Permute\>\"", " ", "->", " ", "False"}], "InlineCode"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"28be4988-f638-d740-8489-9a15f3e7ef75"],
 " the arguments keep the order given, and only the nesting varies."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"535119e3-84c5-a845-94f9-e03aaf750782"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"\"\<IncludeArguments\>\"", " ", "->", " ", "True"}], 
   "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "632416e4-1b46-1344-94ff-bc2b3bdfcfdb"],
 ", the default, also considers equations whose left-hand side is a bare \
argument, which is how an identity such as an absorbing element is found."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"a6a13b19-57dd-954b-8a51-d6cdbf884c6a"],

Cell["\<\
The search is in two stages. Every term is first evaluated on random integer \
arrays, which is fast, and only pairs that agree there are carried forward. \
Terms equal as identities agree on any arrays, so nothing correct is ever \
discarded; what survives is verified on symbolic arrays by expanding the \
difference of the two sides.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"8ebb25c2-dd37-b249-ae29-a5e9e81f082a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The test arrays have order ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["\"\<Order\>\"", "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "32864751-2a11-bb42-bf35-c9e37b186b87"],
 " and every index of length ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["\"\<Dimension\>\"", "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"f13db19c-03e0-5448-8fa3-1385d8d2e52e"],
 ". The order is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Automatic", "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "91f398a6-17c9-c64e-80ab-cec81e52d97c"],
 " by default, meaning the smallest order from 1 to 5 on which every \
operation actually evaluates, which is what makes a set of matrix operations \
and a set of 3-array operations both work without being told. The dimension \
is 2 by default."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"7154d110-73da-a844-8e7d-95bf9c393d48"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "An identity is verified on symbolic arrays of that dimension, so it is \
proved at that dimension rather than at every dimension; raising ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["\"\<Dimension\>\"", "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"5caba364-fc50-1641-9448-1357bc2859b7"],
 " checks it more widely. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["\"\<Trials\>\"", "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "472c7fa8-f32e-3b4d-ae0b-7ded044b47b3"],
 " sets of random arrays are used for the numerical screen."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"19ffe86d-f961-bb4f-969c-07802cfb2bda"],

Cell["\<\
A nesting that the operations cannot evaluate is dropped rather than \
compared, which is what lets an operation returning a scalar, such as a \
trace, sit at the top of a term without its appearing anywhere it cannot. If \
no nesting evaluates at all, FindArrayEquations::eval is produced and $Failed \
returned, so that being unable to test anything is never reported as having \
found no equations.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"9b180e0a-23c8-bf41-a6ba-bbb029600041"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "An operation of arity one is the exception to the rule that a term uses \
exactly the given arguments: it takes a term to a term of the same shape, so \
it may be applied over and over at any point without consuming one. Every \
term is offered wrapped in up to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["\"\<UnaryDepth\>\"", "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"2f10a0b3-763a-8342-9e48-3715a0750ee2"],
 " nested unary operations, two by default, which is what brings a law such \
as the involutivity of a transpose within reach. Setting it to 0 leaves the \
unary operations out of the terms altogether."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"c90c84f9-b874-334b-8204-afda935aa87f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "With ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"\"\<Reduce\>\"", " ", "->", " ", "True"}], "InlineCode"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"e685243f-9173-6641-b92c-d0f8aaa1c36b"],
 ", the default, an equation that follows from the others is not reported. \
Each equation is read as a schema, its arguments standing for arbitrary \
terms, and is turned into a rewrite rule that fires only when it makes a term \
strictly smaller; equations whose two sides then rewrite to a common form are \
consequences of the ones already kept, and are dropped. Without this a single \
involution would be reported over again for every term it can be buried in. \
",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"\"\<Reduce\>\"", " ", "->", " ", "False"}], "InlineCode"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"e80099be-5fe1-6940-aaf5-aadd6a57f74e"],
 " gives every equation found."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"a856d605-1c1b-5e4e-ad31-61bd347682e1"],

Cell["\<\
What survives the reduction is a basis, not a canonical set: which of several \
equivalent forms of a law is kept depends on the order the equations are \
considered in, and the reduction is by rewriting alone, so a consequence that \
rewriting cannot reach is still reported.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"fee92f59-58de-5d45-83a0-6a6f9ea20e70"],

Cell["\<\
The operations must be defined so that they do not evaluate on bare symbols, \
which is what definitions with _List or array patterns achieve. Otherwise the \
terms collapse before they can be compared.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"dd7a30e8-aca0-c147-825d-800be9251d4f"],

Cell["Examples", "PrimaryExamplesSection",ExpressionUUID->"374aed41-49d0-5748-bdd2-b49fca3a11d0"],

Cell["\<\
Ordinary matrix multiplication on three generic arguments finds \
associativity, and nothing else:\
\>", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"eb779a6c-5a5f-0d4e-b914-ef5da8298e94"],

Cell["\<\
dot[u_List, w_List] := Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{u, w}, \"ij,jk->ik\"];
FindArrayEquations[{dot}, {a1, a2, a3}, \"Order\" -> 2, \"Dimension\" -> 3]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"7bf8a27c-454c-4544-b932-028fbce89f7e"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
    RowBox[{"a1", ",", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a3"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
   RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2"}], "]"}], ",", "a3"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",\
ExpressionUUID->"d983392c-d094-544f-8e05-96b292fc2150"],

Cell["\<\
Keeping the arguments in the order given makes no difference here, since \
associativity does not permute them:\
\>", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"2ae2a316-67a4-fb41-8795-aa55020a2276"],

Cell["\<\
FindArrayEquations[{dot}, {a1, a2, a3}, \"Order\" -> 2, \"Dimension\" -> 3, \
\"Permute\" -> False]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"86b4f6c5-f39a-af4b-8468-54628daad7b8"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
    RowBox[{"a1", ",", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a3"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
   RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2"}], "]"}], ",", "a3"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",\
ExpressionUUID->"0002bdf3-4556-b24f-b878-472626edc6fd"],

Cell["\<\
The Fish product of three 3-arrays satisfies a twisted form of ternary \
associativity:\
\>", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"2e47adb6-3118-f741-9741-c6dae474649c"],

Cell["\<\
Fish[u_List, w_List, t_List] := Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{u, w, t}, \
\"ijp,qrp,qrk->ijk\"];
FindArrayEquations[{Fish}, {a1, a2, a3, a4, a5}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"b7d61315-7234-4441-8953-ce3ff6d80546"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2", ",", 
      RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
       RowBox[{"a3", ",", "a4", ",", "a5"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", 
      RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
       RowBox[{"a4", ",", "a3", ",", "a2"}], "]"}], ",", "a5"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2", ",", 
      RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
       RowBox[{"a3", ",", "a4", ",", "a5"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
       RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2", ",", "a3"}], "]"}], ",", "a4", ",", "a5"}], 
     "]"}]}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"0116a6c2-e6e2-e64f-a77f-\
c12d527c8c90"],

Cell["\<\
The Bhattacharya-Mesner product, by contrast, satisfies no equation at all on \
five arguments:\
\>", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"b80ff04f-e80d-9847-bd31-817fe6c87277"],

Cell["\<\
BM[u_List, w_List, t_List] := Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{u, w, t}, \
\"ijp,ipk,pjk->ijk\"];
FindArrayEquations[{BM}, {a1, a2, a3, a4, a5}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"3a66288f-d229-a54b-a78b-71699de9f53c"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"46a13350-bc02-364f-9e15-19a4b99e910b"],

Cell["An arity that cannot be read off a definition must be given:", \
"ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"90df4fcc-25b0-ab4a-ba35-66135bfcc020"],

Cell["\<\
FindArrayEquations[{dot -> 2}, {a1, a2, a3}, \"Order\" -> 2, \"Dimension\" -> \
3]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"be5bc6de-09e9-9f48-a237-ed6bb302e2e1"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
    RowBox[{"a1", ",", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a3"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
   RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2"}], "]"}], ",", "a3"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",\
ExpressionUUID->"e081ef0c-f39c-2f45-90cf-a8bf9d67857d"],

Cell["\<\
Several operations may be searched together. A transpose and a matrix product \
give the law that transposing a product reverses it, and the law that \
transposing twice does nothing:\
\>", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"beb08c8b-26be-7d4d-8868-a96c44887d70"],

Cell["\<\
perp[u_List] := Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{u}, \"ij->ji\"];
FindArrayEquations[{perp, dot}, {a1, a2}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"11f6d6a7-c47d-ac4e-962f-b2d3c00fe271"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"a1", "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "]"}]}]}], "}"}]], "Output",Expression\
UUID->"3c5c2570-3519-494f-98b9-e7c845dcc735"],

Cell["\<\
Holding the arguments in the order given leaves only the involutivity, since \
the other law moves them:\
\>", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"54ca846c-a5b1-a147-9239-4be0f7654e28"],

Cell["\<\
FindArrayEquations[{perp, dot}, {a1, a2}, \"Permute\" -> False]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"896580ab-c309-2e49-ad73-a746e551b1f0"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"a1", "\[Equal]", 
   RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID\
->"62f85387-f717-8646-9b5d-8cc58c61e982"],

Cell["\<\
Every consequence of those two laws holds as well, and without the reduction \
each is reported in its own right:\
\>", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"2755c15b-d9c5-0a4a-b913-9114704dabb7"],

Cell["\<\
Length @ FindArrayEquations[{perp, dot}, {a1, a2}, \"Reduce\" -> False]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"950df1b1-9c52-9841-9bf1-b0e8cd76fb2b"],

Cell[BoxData["115"], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"c6fec8ad-04fa-2a4a-9379-75f6a946e5c7"],

Cell["\<\
The involutivity needs the transpose twice over, so it is out of reach when \
the unary operations may not be nested \[LongDash] and without it the \
remaining law is reported in each of its forms, there being nothing left to \
rewrite one into another:\
\>", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"0a690fea-2b68-2b4c-bc98-b7c83f878f68"],

Cell["FindArrayEquations[{perp, dot}, {a1, a2}, \"UnaryDepth\" -> 1]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"efefcdaf-87d2-5a4f-8b60-799e78fac246"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", 
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a2", ",", 
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a2", ",", 
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a1", ",", 
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], ",", "a2"}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}], ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}], ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], ",", "a2"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2"}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]}]}], "}"}]], "Output",\
ExpressionUUID->"d1379695-08df-1e48-b97d-93b409e42c45"],

Cell["\<\
A trace and a matrix product give the cyclic property of the trace. The trace \
returns a scalar, so the nestings that would feed it back into a product \
simply drop out:\
\>", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"ae5ca3ef-ebd7-7c42-a59d-740c58a7576e"],

Cell["\<\
trace[u_List] := Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{u}, \"pp->\"];
FindArrayEquations[{trace, dot}, {a1, a2}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"491878f9-6f83-064f-9be6-7088c570ae9e"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"trace", "[", 
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2"}], "]"}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
   RowBox[{"trace", "[", 
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUI\
D->"052b0a15-20dd-6f41-8d24-0350a39ee16e"],

Cell["\<\
Each of those inferred an order of 2 from the operations themselves. Forcing \
a shape they cannot take reports as much, rather than returning no equations:\
\
\>", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"99720ad0-d1dd-554f-b98e-be0e4e15d9bd"],

Cell["\<\
Quiet @ FindArrayEquations[{perp, dot}, {a1, a2}, \"Order\" -> 4]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"87aca1f4-5b7d-2c43-9259-36e34f9e6393"],

Cell[BoxData["$Failed"], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"12f64553-6f67-7a41-b352-67f3970bcb50"],

Cell["Source", "NotesSection",ExpressionUUID->"33e2b327-0b3d-4643-8d3d-c6e80d5c5346"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Defined in ",
 StyleBox["src/ArrayAlgebra.wl",
  FontFamily->"Courier",
  FontWeight->"Plain"],
 ", section ",
 "\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]",
 "FindArrayEquations",
 "\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"c8208755-f980-be4d-9207-45002bd2fd8d"],

Cell["See Also", "SeeAlsoSection",ExpressionUUID->"cf4cc7a6-cd41-cc40-bd7e-bb0554c28038"],

